The attraction you're describing is **La Bufadora** — a marine blowhole on the **Punta Banda Peninsula** in Baja California, Mexico, roughly 27 km (17 mi) south of Ensenada. It's a natural sea cave at the base of a cliff: waves and air get forced into the cave, and the pressure shoots seawater up through a hole in the rock with a loud roar, often reaching 60–100 feet (about 20–30 m) high.

It's widely billed as the world's second-largest marine geyser (some sources call it the largest in North America, with the top spot going to a blowhole in Oahu, Hawaii). Travelers note the spray is best at high tide, when the waves are strongest, and there's a cliffside boardwalk with views over the Pacific according to [Luggage and Lipstick](w3) and [Be My Travel Muse](w4).

