The aquarium you're thinking of is the **Alaska SeaLife Center** in Seward, Alaska. It's the state's only public aquarium and is explicitly structured as a combined public exhibit, marine research, wildlife rescue/rehabilitation, and education facility — it grew out of the Exxon Valdez oil spill response and still runs a marine mammal and seabird rescue program alongside its research labs and public galleries.

It sits at 301 Railway Avenue on the Seward waterfront, right next to the Seward Marine Center, and is well covered in travel writing (18 blog mentions in the database). Note that Alaska also has a smaller aquarium-style facility, the Sitka Sound Science Center, which pairs a touch-tank aquarium with the historic Sheldon Jackson Hatchery — but the SeaLife Center is the one that uniquely combines public viewing with research, rescue and education.

