rows.final:
**Bjargarsteinn Mathús** is the pick — a small harbour-side restaurant at 15 Sólvellir in Grundarfjörður, about 400 m from the town centre and looking straight across the water at Kirkjufell. It's the only real sit-down dinner spot in town, and travellers consistently rate it highly: one blog describes a five-star TripAdvisor restaurant in a "marvelous harbor setting facing Mount Kirkjufell," where the fish soup, deep-fried seaweed and dried cod stood out, along with the chance to try fermented shark and Brennivín ([More Time to Travel](w10)). Others called the local seafood, fermented shark and puffin on the menu "pricey but amazing" ([Wonderstruck Wanderings](w9)), and one couple, arriving after the kitchen had closed on a Sunday, were still served the seafood soup and red fish and called it one of the best meals of their whole Iceland trip ([Wonderstruck Wanderings](w7)). Phone +354 438 6770, website bjargarsteinn.is.

Two lighter backups in the same block if you want something casual: **Kaffi Emil**, a café/coffee house right by the church square, and **Mæstro – Street Food** on Hamrahlíð for quick bites. Note the warning from [Wonderstruck Wanderings](w7): on Sundays in particular, most restaurants and even grocery shops in Grundarfjörður shut well before 7 pm, so it's worth calling ahead or arriving early in the evening. For a sunset dinner, the walk down to Bjargarsteinn also puts you close to the Kirkjufell viewpoint.

